      "id": 1,
      "text": "Richland began as a secret Manhattan Project town in 1943 and became an official city only in 1958.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richland%2C_Washington",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Many streets honor engineers—John Frank Stevens, Sylvanus Thayer, and others—highlighting the town’s scientific heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        3,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richland%2C_Washington",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Reach Museum tells the story of the Hanford Site, Ice Age floods, and the Columbia Basin’s history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richland%2C_Washington",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "LIGO, the Laser Interferometer Gravitational‑Wave Observatory, sits in Richland and helped detect ripples in spacetime.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.visittri-cities.com/about-the-area/cities/richland/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Richland lies where the Yakima and Columbia Rivers meet, creating a unique confluence that shaped its development.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richland%2C_Washington",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "After WWII, the original Hanford workers’ camp north of Richland closed, marking the town’s transition from wartime to peacetime.",
